WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.739
Michael Hauge: That’s pretty much for both
the outer journey and the inner journey level,

00:00:03.739 --> 00:00:06.919
how Stage 1 and Stage 2 work.

00:00:06.919 --> 00:00:11.750
Now we come to the second key turning point
in what I call “The Change of Plans.”

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:17.230
This is the moment when the hero has now formulated
the goal they want to achieve.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:22.130
In his case, he’s going to get the best
plea bargain he can get for these defendants

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:23.980
and they begin pursuing it.

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:29.289
Now the point to notice if you’re a screenwriter
or a filmmaker or even if you’re a novelist,

00:00:29.289 --> 00:00:35.530
that whatever the outer motivation, whatever
this visible goal, your hero is going to pursue,

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:36.850
that defines your movie.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:42.080
Your hero shouldn’t start actually pursuing
it and taking action to cross that finish

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.150
line until the beginning go Act 2.

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:51.350
You’ve got to take entire 25% of your screenplay
just to build up to your hero taking those

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:52.450
first steps.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:56.160
Everything up to now has been him gathering
information to figure out “What are we going

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:57.160
to do?”

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:01.630
It’s only when he takes that first step
of going to The Bay that he is now moving

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:02.720
toward the goal.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.799
And this is Stage 3 in what I call Progress
because his plan is “Okay I’m going to

00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:11.049
get the facts and I’m going to juggle them
around and figure out a way that I can get

00:01:11.049 --> 00:01:16.479
them off with the least sentence possible”
and that is when he meets another key character

00:01:16.479 --> 00:01:18.979
in the story and that is his Nemesis.

00:01:18.979 --> 00:01:24.909
That’s my term for the person who is at
cross-purposes with the hero.

00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:28.439
If the reflection [character] is there to
help the hero, then the nemesis is going to

00:01:28.439 --> 00:01:31.290
stop the hero from achieving his goal.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:32.359
He’s the one in opposition.

00:01:32.359 --> 00:01:34.000
He’s the villain.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.030
In this case he’s more like a villain but
he could be an opponent if it’s a sport’s

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:41.389
story, or it could be a rival if it’s a
love story or a romantic comedy.

00:01:41.389 --> 00:01:44.689
But it’s somebody who goes against the villain.

00:01:44.689 --> 00:01:48.220
And when we meet Jessup (Col. Nathan R. Jessup
- that’s the Jack Nicholson character),

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.049
a few things are apparent immediately.

00:01:51.049 --> 00:01:55.299
One is, he is way way more powerful than (Tom
Cruise’s character - Lt. Daniel) Kaffee

00:01:55.299 --> 00:01:56.299
is.

00:01:56.299 --> 00:01:57.600
This is a must!

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:03.009
When you’re writing a screenplay or a story
of any kind, you always want your nemesis

00:02:03.009 --> 00:02:08.220
to exceed your hero in their power and abilities
and strengths and so on.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:13.970
It must seem like it’s going to be impossible
for your hero to overcome the Nemesis.

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:18.040
So Jessup has all the power, all the rank,
he puts Kaffee down.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:24.470
Kaffee is sort of meek with him and shy and
just wants to get out of the situation.

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:31.629
He seems almost wimpy in the face of Nathan
Jessup, which is exactly as it should be because

00:02:31.629 --> 00:02:32.909
now we’re involved.

00:02:32.909 --> 00:02:35.560
Now we realize the conflict is big.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:39.810
And the bigger the conflict the more our emotional
involvement is in the story.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:45.860
So he starts working and working and working
toward (Kaffee does) figuring out or putting

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:49.769
together defense for them.

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:54.520
Now on the inner journey level, remember he’s
got this tug-of-war going on be between his

00:02:54.520 --> 00:03:01.870
identity, his safe persona that keeps him
stuck in a place that is familiar in his comfort

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:05.180
zone but he’s not going to lead to any fulfillment.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:09.481
And (by the way) I don’t think I mentioned
this, the rule is, if the hero doesn’t move

00:03:09.481 --> 00:03:14.260
into his essence, if the hero is not able
to shed his identity and have the courage

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:18.590
to live his truth, he cannot achieve the goal.

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:23.100
He doesn’t realize that at the point of
the story, he thinks he’ll pursue this goal

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:25.940
so he can keep his identity in place.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:29.769
But it will always follow because you (as
the writer) are going to make it this way.

00:03:29.769 --> 00:03:34.430
But unless the hero finds the courage to leave
his identity, he’s not going to get that

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:35.439
goal.

00:03:35.439 --> 00:03:41.550
So now, your hero is vacillating, moving into
his essence a bit because he’s got to in

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:43.030
order to move closer to the goal.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:47.370
But that becomes very scary because he’s
lost his armor or his emotional protection

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:51.150
is gone so then he’ll retreat and go back
into his identity.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:55.550
So you’ll see when you watch the movie (or
if you watch the movie again) as you watch

00:03:55.550 --> 00:04:00.319
this during this stretch, Kaffee is at times
going to seem like a really good lawyer.

00:04:00.319 --> 00:04:03.960
He’s going to do smart things he’s going
to figure out things, he’s going to know

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:07.540
how to manipulate things (not Kaffee but some
of the other characters).

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:12.989
He’s coming up with ideas, other times he
just wants to back off, get a plea bargain

00:04:12.989 --> 00:04:18.139
or whatever until finally (when he realizes
his defendants are not cooperating at all)

00:04:18.139 --> 00:04:21.340
he says “I can’t understand these guys,
I’m going to quit.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:24.150
I’m going to get the lawyer replaced.”

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:30.680
And he makes that threat right before we reach
the next key turning point, what I call The

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:31.680
Point of No Return.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:32.680
Question for the Viewers: What is the Point
of No Return in Aaron Sorkin’s movie A FEW

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:32.682
GOOD MEN?

